A||||4||beer_stud_21||Been sitting 4 years.  What do i check on?||||1||
Z||000000||beer_stud_21||02-04-2001||06:18 PM||||Hey all, <P>Got my first fiero last night.  I had to tow her home cuz she isnt running under her own power.  (Dist problem)  She cranks, and gets gas but thats all I know so far.  My question is what kind of things should I be checking on since this car has been sitting out in the hawaiian weather for about 4 years.   Change the Oil, check belts...  what else?  Up till now I havnt been a mechanic, but I finally have a project car of my own.  Woohoo   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks all, and you can see pics and get a little more info from my post in general chat.<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||02-04-2001||07:02 PM||||Check Everything. Change the oil. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||02-04-2001||09:05 PM|||| get rid of gas it's not gas anymore, with new gas add enjector cleaner. ck battery need water at best lucky if not dead,clean conections on it.if runs ck tires, brakes work?coolant and brake fluid should be changed some time soon. ck for leaks in hoses or pipes under car good luck ray||||reg||1
Z||000003||GarethWright||02-05-2001||08:22 PM||||Its probably worth the money to get the Engine and Transmission flushed to get rid of anything that may have settled in 4 years, thats done at the same time as the oil change.  I did this when I got mine, it Hadn't been sitting, but it made a World of difference.  Have fun with it and welcome to the club!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jake_Dragon||02-05-2001||09:34 PM||||Found this on a mailing list:<P>I hope that the new Fiero is a good one<P>Clean the car, engine and all components.<BR>Start with fuel: Replace the fuel filter and clean the fuel system. (I<BR>usually replace the injector(s) but this depends on funding. A good<BR>cleaning will take out most of the gunk in the lines and in the injectors.<BR>I also like to drop the tank and clean it and replace the fuel pump and<BR>sender but then again...)<BR>Then go with air: Replace the air filter, PCV valve, and fuel canister<BR>filter, making sure that every thing is cleaned up. Check the wet and dry<BR>compression ratio for all the cylinders. (Good compression holds at about<BR>120-130 psi.)<BR>Then check for good fire: Replace your spark plugs, wires, cap, rotor,<BR>ignition module, ignition coil, and pick up coil. (I would replace your<BR>battery and alternator as well! $130 for a ignition module is a bit steep<BR>are you sure the part quoted was not the computer?)<BR>Then go with fluids: Change the oil and filter. Check the coolant<BR>level, (I would personally replace the coolant with a flush and back flush).<BR>Replace the coolant caps (front and rear) and the thermostat. Check the<BR>transmission fluid and clutch fluid.<BR>Then check wiring: Look for frayed and/or broken wires throughout the<BR>car. Even look for patch jobs (i.e. splices) which are not done well. (If<BR>you do find a bad splice then take off the solderless connector or<BR>electrical tape and then solder and heat shrink the wire back together.)<BR>Make sure all the connections are tight and that the connectors are not<BR>broken or cracked. (if they are you can replace just the plastic house<BR>rather than the entire wire set.)<BR>Check the functionality of the engine: Check and reset the timing if<BR>necessary. Check the valve lash and make sure that the top end is working<BR>within specs. If you have time check the timing gears.<BR>Then check your exhaust system: Check the pipes, manifolds. Make sure<BR>the EGR is working well and that the EGR tube is in good condition.<BR>(Replacement of the EGR is a good idea anyway!)<BR>Then check your cables and linkages: Lube and replace as needed.<BR>Then check the belts, hoses, vacuum lines and replace as needed.<BR>Little touches like replacing fuses and bulbs is a good idea as well but<BR>not necessary.<BR>There is more to check but the above will take care of a lot of woes new<BR>owners have after purchasing any used car. When finished correctly the<BR>Fiero should run very well. My 84 SE is a back up vehicle because it is<BR>more reliable than my girl friend's newer car. And the story of it's<BR>revival is a long and fun one to say the least!<P>After all this, then you can move on to suspension, breaks, tires,<BR>lights and fixtures, steering, interior, exterior... But for reliability<BR>when you don't know the "real" history behind the car then it would be wise<BR>to check EVERYTHING! Basically when you get finished you end up with an<BR>almost new car anyway!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Glenn Leber||||reg||1
